--- !u!114 &192708778
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 192708776}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c5f73fd4d9310bd478847f08afba84ad, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::QuizBench
  kind: 5
  amber: {r: 1, g: 0.69803923, b: 0.24705882, a: 1}
  bright: {r: 1, g: 0.8862745, b: 0.65882355, a: 1}
  dim: {r: 0.65882355, g: 0.4392157, b: 0.12156863, a: 1}
  teal: {r: 0.24705882, g: 0.8784314, b: 0.70980394, a: 1}
  alert: {r: 1, g: 0.41568628, b: 0.2901961, a: 1}
  off: {r: 0.22745098, g: 0.14901961, b: 0.043137256, a: 1}
  questions:
  - question: "What does the X gate do to |0\u27E9?"
    options:
    - "It turns it into |1\u27E9"
    - It leaves it unchanged
    - It makes a 50/50 superposition
    correct: 0
    explain: "X swaps the basis states, so |0\u27E9 becomes |1\u27E9 (entry 7)."
  - question: A qubit in a 50/50 superposition is measured once. What do you see?
    options:
    - Both 0 and 1 at once
    - One result, 0 or 1, each with 50% chance
    - Always 0
    correct: 1
    explain: One measurement gives exactly one result. Never both (entry 4).
  - question: The console shows syndrome 01. Which qubit flipped, and what is the
      fix?
    options:
    - 'q0: apply X to q0'
    - 'q1: apply X to q1'
    - 'q2: apply X to q2'
    - 'No error: no correction'
    correct: 2
    explain: Only the second pair differs, which singles out q2 (entry 17).
  - question: The data reads 010. What is the syndrome?
    options:
    - 00
    - 10
    - 11
    - 01
    correct: 2
    explain: "(0 \u2295 1, 1 \u2295 0) = 11, which points to q1 (entry 16)."
  - question: Why not measure the data qubits directly?
    options:
    - It would collapse any superposition
    - It is too slow
    - Qiskit does not allow it
    correct: 0
    explain: It would collapse any superposition. Parity checks reveal only relationships
      (entry 11).
  - question: Why can this code not fix a phase flip?
    options:
    - It needs more ancillas
    - Parity checks compare bit values only
    - Phase flips are not real errors
    correct: 1
    explain: A phase flip changes a sign, not bit values, so the checks can't see
      it (entry 20).
  progressText: {fileID: 1332065392}
  questionText: {fileID: 1779485000}
  feedbackText: {fileID: 1897548314}
  optionButtons:
  - {fileID: 2147214911}
  - {fileID: 307687628}
  - {fileID: 250603171}
  - {fileID: 1601375582}
  nextButton: {fileID: 1809001109}
